end;
</code></pre>

Q: How can I bitwise-cast a 32-bit float into an integer without using typed arrays? A: <p>You can always construct the bit representation manually, using the logarithm to estimate the exponent, then multiplying the number by an appropriate power of two to extract the mantissa bits.  Something like this:</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// use exponentiation by squaring to avoid precision issues
// when Math.pow(base, n) is defined as Math.exp(n * Math.log(base))
function powi(base, n) {
  if (n &lt; 0)
    return 1 / powi(base, -n);
  if (n === 0)
    return 1;
  if (n % 2 === 0)
    return powi(base * base, n / 2);
  return base * powi(base * base, (n - 1) / 2);
}

var HALF_EPSILON    = powi(2, -53);
var HALF_EPSILON32  = powi(2, -24);

function f32_to_u32(x) {
  if (x !== x)                 /* NaN */
    return 0x7fc00000;
  var signBit = x + (1 / x) &lt; 0 ? 0x80000000 : 0;

  x = Math.abs(x);
  if (x === 0)                 /* zero */
    return signBit;

  var exp, mantf;

  if (x === Infinity) {
    exp = Infinity;
    mantf = 0;
  } else {
    // use the logarithm to estimate the exponent part,
    // then refine the estimate to correct rounding errors     
    exp = Math.floor(Math.log(x) * Math.LOG2E);
    var mantd = x * powi(2, -exp);

    mantd /= HALF_EPSILON;
    while (mantd !== Math.floor(mantd)) {
      mantd *= 2;
      exp--;
    }
    mantd *= HALF_EPSILON;

    while (mantd &gt;= 1) {
      mantd /= 2;
      exp++;
    }

    // round to 32-bit precision
    mantf = Math.round(mantd / HALF_EPSILON32) * HALF_EPSILON32;

    while (mantf &gt;= 1) {
      mantf /= 2;
      exp++;
    }
  }

  // extract the mantissa
  var mant;
  if (exp &gt; 128) {             /* infinities */
    return (signBit | 0x7f800000) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
  } else if (exp &lt;= -126) {    /* denormals */
    mant = mantf / HALF_EPSILON32 * powi(2, 125 + exp);
    exp = -126;
  } else {
    mant = mantf / HALF_EPSILON32;
  }
  mant &amp;= 0x7fffff;
  exp = (exp + 126) &lt;&lt; 23;

  return (signBit | exp | mant) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
}

// test

{
  const f32_to_u32_ref = f =&gt;
    new Uint32Array(new Float32Array([f]).buffer)[0];

  const check = (f, u = f32_to_u32_ref(f)) =&gt; {
    if (f32_to_u32(f) !== u) {
      throw `f32_to_u32(${f}) = 0x${
        f32_to_u32(f).toString(16)} ≠ 0x${
        u.toString(16)}`;
    }
  };

  check(0);
  check(-0);
  check(1);
  check(1.5);
  check(NaN);
  check(Infinity);
  check(-Infinity);

  for (var i = 0; i &lt; 64; ++i) {
    const r = Math.random();
    check(r);
  }

  const u32 = new Uint32Array([0]);
  const f32 = new Float32Array(u32.buffer);

  for (var x = 0; x &lt; 0xfffff; ++x) {
    u32[0] = ((((x &lt;&lt; 24) &gt;&gt; 24) &amp; 0xffffff) | ((x &amp; 0xfff00) &lt;&lt; 20)) &gt;&gt;&gt; 0;
    f32[0] = f32[0]; // normalize NaNs
    check(f32[0], u32[0]);
  }

  console.info('all tests passed');
}</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>I can safely say this works in IE6, because I tested it in IE5.</p>

Q: What is the problem namespaces in XML trying to solve? A: <ol>
<li><p>Browsers only parse HTML, SVG and MathML. The HTML specification has special rules for how to parse SVG and MathML in HTML (and lots of special rules as to how to parse HTML itself). If we wanted to add some other thing then we'd have to all agree what the rules are for parsing it.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Parsing of XML is entirely generic. A parser does not need to know any context special rules. Nobody has to form a committee to determine what rules to use to parse anything else.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XML came before SVG/MathML parsing in HMTL. Really because XHTML never really took off in browsers.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XSD can work with any XML containing non-XHTML, non-SVG and non-MathML data. HTML can only contain those 3 things.</p>
</li>
<li><p>XML is used for lots of things that have nothing to do with browsers and where it contains no HTML, SVG or MathML at all.</p>
</li>
<li><p>A concrete example would be anything other than SVG or MathML within HTML. The parser wouldn't know how to parse it so it would treat it all as unknown HTML elements which may not be what you wanted.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>In terms of parsing let's say you data about dogs.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dog breed=&quot;terrier&quot;...&gt;
   &lt;bark pitch=&quot;&quot; duration=&quot;&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/dog&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and other data about trees</p>
<pre><code>&lt;tree species=&quot;pine&quot; ...&gt;
   &lt;bark texture=&quot;smooth&quot; colour=&quot;brown&quot;.../&gt;
&lt;/tree&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>and we want to extend our dog dataset to contain information about which kind of tree each breed of dog likes best.</p>
<pre><code>&lt;dog breed=&quot;terrier&quot;...&gt;
   &lt;bark pitch=&quot;&quot; duration=&quot;&quot;/&gt;
   &lt;likes&gt;
     &lt;tree species=&quot;pine&quot; ...&gt;
       &lt;bark texture=&quot;smooth&quot; colour=&quot;brown&quot;.../&gt;
     &lt;/tree&gt;
   &lt;/likes&gt;
&lt;/dog&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>Now the meaning of bark depends on the context i.e. what its parent is rather than having a single fixed meaning throughout the file.</p>
<p>Namespaces mark that context change. The two bark elements would be in different namespaces use that to validate all the attributes of the different bark elements.</p>
